{"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"3168d6f1","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.003013,"end_time":"2026-11-17T14:00:06.009079+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-17T14:00:06.006066+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# MAPS (authors' code) on multiple datasets: 5-fold F1 evaluation\n","This notebook runs the **official MAPS model code** (Shaban et al., Nat Commun 2024; repo `mahmoodlab/MAPS`, commit `72d4bc8`) on your cell tables.\n","\n","**What is the authors' exact code here**\n","- The files `datasets.py`, `networks.py`, `trainer.py`, `predictor.py` are copied **verbatim** from the repo (cells 3-7). The model, normalization, sampler, loss, early stopping and checkpointing are all theirs.\n","- Training settings default to the values in the authors' `CRC_CODEX.sh` (batch 128, lr 0.001, dropout 0.25, 500/100/50 epochs/min-epochs/patience, seed 7325111).\n","- Cell 8 applies **2 tiny compatibility patches** (see that cell). Without them the authors' code crashes on current NumPy/PyTorch.\n","\n","**What is not theirs (they never released it for your datasets)**\n","- Turning each dataset's table into MAPS format (markers + cell size + `cell_label`) and the 5-fold split. I mirror their approach: folds are made **by patient/region/spot** using their size-balancing rule, and the training part is split 80/20 into train/valid (like their `cHL_CODEX.py`), so the test fold is never used for early stopping.\n","\n","**How to use:** attach your dataset to the notebook as Kaggle input, then **Run All**. The notebook finds the data, detects the columns, trains the 5 folds and saves the results. Nothing needs editing unless the *Check* cell shows a wrong column (then fill `OVERRIDES` in cell 5). Finished folds are skipped if you re-run after a crash/timeout."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"66ea4016","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.002254,"end_time":"2026-11-17T14:00:06.013998+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-17T14:00:06.01